Requirements :

- Log levels : Should be able to log different category of logs like Info, Error, and Debug.
- Multiple outputs: Should be able to write logs in various destinations like Console, File, and Database.
- Configurable: Should provide flexibility to add/remove logging levels and destinations.


Design Pattern Used:
- Singleton Pattern: To ensure there is only one instance of logging throughout the application.
- Chain of Responsibility (CoR) : CoR will be used to pass or delegate the message through a chain of handlers for 
filtering and processing.
- Observer Pattern: To write in different destinations. As soon as we have something to write we notify all the 
relevant destinations to log the message. In simple terms, to braodcast log message to multiple destinations.


Entities:-
1. LogLevel : A enum value of log levels. 
2. LoggerHandle: An abstract class to define the chain of handlers. Here, we can see one logger process the message
and pass the message to another logger.

It’s concrete classes will be

InfoLogger: Log the Info logs.
ErrorLogger: Log the Error logs.
DebugLogger: Log the Debug logs.

3. Logger : The main class which is exposed to the application. The application will call the info, error or debug method
of the class to log the respective message. I have made the class singleton so that at any given time we have only one 
instance of the logger.
4. LogManager: It handles the chaining and all all the observer/destination for a specific log level. I have internally 
implemented the handler chaining and adding of observer/destinations. We can also modify the code to use the dynamic chaining 
and dynamic addition of the destinations.
5. LogObserver: An interface which defines the log method. We will notify all the observer via this log method.
6. LogSinkSubject: This class contains the method for adding more observers and also notify all the observers about a log message.

Some of the observer are

FileLogger: log the message in a file.
ConsoleLogger: log the message in a console.
DatabaseLogger: log the message in a Database.
7. LoggerApplication


Workflow will be like:

1. Logger acts a starting point for the application. With the help of Logger class, we create an instance of the logger and using the 
instance we call the info, error or debug method to log the message.
2. Log message are pass through the handlers and then proceed to the destinations.
3. Each handler processed the logs based on the level condition and notify all its observer or destinations and then passed it to
the next handler for processing.


More info - https://medium.com/@say2ankitgupta/low-level-design-of-logging-framework-ffaae7c701ee



public enum LogLevel {
    INFO(1), DEBUG(3), ERROR(2);

    private final int level;

    LogLevel (int value) {
        this.level = value;
    }

    public int getLevel() {
        return level;
    }
}

public enum SinkType {
    CONSOLE, FILE, DATABASE
}




public interface LogObserver {
    void log(String message);
}

public class FileLogger implements LogObserver{
    public void log(String message) {
        System.out.println("Logging into file: " + message);
    }

    // override so duplicate observer is not added for same level
    @Override
    public boolean equals(Object obj) {
        if (this == obj) return true;
        if (obj == null || getClass() != obj.getClass()) return false;
        return true; // All FileLogger instances are considered equal
    }

    // override so duplicate observer is not added for same level
    @Override
    public int hashCode() {
        return FileLogger.class.hashCode(); // Unique hash code for FileLogger class
    }
}


public class ConsoleLogger implements LogObserver{
    public void log(String message) {
        System.out.println("Logging into Console: " + message);
    }

    // override so duplicate observer is not added for same level
    @Override
    public boolean equals(Object obj) {
        if (this == obj) return true;
        if (obj == null || getClass() != obj.getClass()) return false;
        return true; // All ConsoleLogger instances are considered equal
    }

    @Override
    public int hashCode() {
        return ConsoleLogger.class.hashCode(); // Unique hash code for ConsoleLogger class
    }
}


public class DataBaseLogger implements LogObserver{
    public void log(String message) {
        System.out.println("Logging into database" + message);
    }

    // override so duplicate observer is not added for same level
    @Override
    public boolean equals(Object obj) {
        if (this == obj) return true;
        if (obj == null || getClass() != obj.getClass()) return false;
        return true; // All DataBaseLogger instances are considered equal
    }

    @Override
    public int hashCode() {
        return DataBaseLogger.class.hashCode(); // Unique hash code for DataBaseLogger class
    }
}


public abstract class LoggerHandler {
    private LoggerHandler nextLoggerHandler;
    public LogLevel logLevel;

    public void setNextLoggerHandler(LoggerHandler nextLoggerHandler) {
        this.nextLoggerHandler = nextLoggerHandler;
    }

    public void log(LogLevel level, String message, LogSinkSubject logSinkSubject) {
        if (this.logLevel.getLevel() == level.getLevel()) {
            publishLog(message, logSinkSubject);
        }

        if (nextLoggerHandler != null) {
            nextLoggerHandler.log(level, message, logSinkSubject);
        }
    }

    public abstract void publishLog(String message, LogSinkSubject logSinkSubject);
}


public class InfoLogger extends LoggerHandler{

    public InfoLogger(LogLevel level) {
        this.logLevel = level;
    }

    public void publishLog(String message, LogSinkSubject logSinkSubject) {
        String msg = "Info: " + message;
        logSinkSubject.notifyObservers(LogLevel.INFO, msg);
    }
}


public class DebugLogger extends LoggerHandler{
    public DebugLogger(LogLevel level) {
        this.logLevel = level;
    }

    public void publishLog(String message, LogSinkSubject logSinkSubject) {
        String msg = "Debug: " + message;
        logSinkSubject.notifyObservers(LogLevel.DEBUG, msg);
    }
}


public class ErrorLogger extends LoggerHandler{
    public ErrorLogger(LogLevel level) {
        this.logLevel = level;
    }

    public void publishLog(String message, LogSinkSubject logSinkSubject) {
        String msg = "Error: " + message;
        logSinkSubject.notifyObservers(LogLevel.ERROR, msg);
    }
}

public class LogManager {

    public static LoggerHandler buildLoggerChain() {
        LoggerHandler infoLogger = new InfoLogger(LogLevel.INFO);
        LoggerHandler errorLogger = new ErrorLogger(LogLevel.ERROR);
        LoggerHandler debugLogger = new DebugLogger(LogLevel.DEBUG);

        infoLogger.setNextLoggerHandler(errorLogger);
        errorLogger.setNextLoggerHandler(debugLogger);

        return infoLogger;
    }

public class LogSinkSubject {
    private final Map<LogLevel, List<LogObserver>> observers = new HashMap<>();

//    public void addObserver(LogLevel level, LogObserver logObserver) {
//        List<LogObserver> logObservers = observers.getOrDefault(level, new ArrayList<>());
//        logObservers.add(logObserver);
//        observers.put(level, logObservers);
//    }

// Add an observer, preventing duplicates for the same LogLevel
    public void addObserver(LogLevel level, LogObserver logObserver) {
        List<LogObserver> logObservers = observers.getOrDefault(level, new ArrayList<>());

        // Check if the observer is already in the list to avoid duplicates
        if (!logObservers.contains(logObserver)) {
            logObservers.add(logObserver);
            observers.put(level, logObservers);  // Update the map with the new observer list
            System.out.println("Added observer: " + logObserver.getClass().getSimpleName() + " for level: " + level.getLevel());
        } else {
            System.out.println("Observer already exists for level: " + level.getLevel());
        }
    }


    public void notifyObservers(LogLevel level, String message) {
        for (Map.Entry<LogLevel, List<LogObserver>> entry: observers.entrySet()) {
           if (entry.getKey().getLevel() == level.getLevel()) {
               entry.getValue().forEach(observers -> observers.log(message));
           }
        }
    }
}


    public static LogSinkSubject buildLogSinkSubject(LogLevel logLevel, SinkType type, LogSinkSubject logSinkSubject) {
        switch (type) {
            case SinkType.CONSOLE -> logSinkSubject.addObserver(logLevel, new ConsoleLogger());
            case SinkType.FILE -> logSinkSubject.addObserver(logLevel, new FileLogger());
            case SinkType.DATABASE -> logSinkSubject.addObserver(logLevel, new DataBaseLogger());
            default -> throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid LogSinkType");
        }
//        logSinkSubject.addObserver(LogLevel.INFO, new ConsoleLogger());
//        logSinkSubject.addObserver(LogLevel.ERROR, new FileLogger());
//        logSinkSubject.addObserver(LogLevel.DEBUG, new DataBaseLogger());

        return logSinkSubject;
    }
}

public class Logger {
    private volatile static Logger loggerInstance;
    private volatile static LoggerHandler loggerHandler;
    private volatile static LogSinkSubject logSinkSubject;

    private Logger() {}

    public static Logger getLoggerInstance() {
        if(loggerInstance == null) {
            synchronized (Logger.class) {
                if(loggerInstance == null) {
                    loggerInstance = new Logger();
                    logSinkSubject = new LogSinkSubject();
                    loggerHandler = LogManager.buildLoggerChain();
                }
            }
        }
        return loggerInstance;
    }

    public void addLogSinkSubject(LogLevel logLevel, SinkType sinkType) {
        logSinkSubject = LogManager.buildLogSinkSubject(logLevel, sinkType, logSinkSubject);
    }

    private void logMessage(String message, LogLevel level) {
        loggerHandler.log(level, message, logSinkSubject);
    }

    public void info(String message) {
        logMessage(message, LogLevel.INFO);
    }

    public void error(String message) {
        logMessage(message, LogLevel.ERROR);
    }

    public void debug(String message) {
        logMessage(message, LogLevel.DEBUG);
    }
}

public class LoggerApplication {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Logger logger = Logger.getLoggerInstance();
        logger.addLogSinkSubject(LogLevel.INFO, SinkType.FILE);
        logger.addLogSinkSubject(LogLevel.INFO, SinkType.DATABASE);
        logger.addLogSinkSubject(LogLevel.INFO, SinkType.CONSOLE);
        logger.addLogSinkSubject(LogLevel.DEBUG, SinkType.CONSOLE);
        logger.addLogSinkSubject(LogLevel.DEBUG, SinkType.FILE);
        logger.addLogSinkSubject(LogLevel.ERROR, SinkType.CONSOLE);
        // check that no duplicate observer should be added again
        logger.addLogSinkSubject(LogLevel.INFO, SinkType.FILE);
        logger.addLogSinkSubject(LogLevel.INFO, SinkType.DATABASE);
        logger.addLogSinkSubject(LogLevel.INFO, SinkType.CONSOLE);
        logger.addLogSinkSubject(LogLevel.DEBUG, SinkType.CONSOLE);
        logger.addLogSinkSubject(LogLevel.DEBUG, SinkType.FILE);
        logger.addLogSinkSubject(LogLevel.ERROR, SinkType.CONSOLE);
        logger.info("This is info");
        logger.error("This is error");
        logger.debug("This is debug");
    }
}



